package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneIntroduction2 is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("experiment/Scenes/intro.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews. An experiment, so it stays
        // out of search: the real lesson is the one to find.
        SetDescription("An introduction to scene editing: what a scene editor is, where it came from, 2D and 3D scenes, and x, y and z coordinates.")
        SetSearchable(false)

        Title("Introduction to Scene Editing")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("In this tutorial, we learn about scene editing. Across this series, we will
look at how to make scenes, use them, load them, and make games with them. This first page explains
what a scene editor is and where the idea came from. It also covers how 2D and 3D scenes are used, how
positions in a scene are described with x, y and z coordinates, and why Quorum Studio's scene editor
can be used with a screen reader, a keyboard or a mouse.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Explain what a scene editor is and where scene editors came from.")
        ListItem("Compare what 2D and 3D scenes are used for.")
        ListItem("Describe the position of an object using x, y and z coordinates.")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Think of a game you know well. Pick one level or one room. How many things
are in it: walls, trees, coins, enemies, lights? Now imagine you had to type the exact position of every
one of them, as numbers. How long would that take, and how would you check you got them right?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Introduction/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_HISTORY_OF_COMPUTING_38)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_1)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_CAREER_EXPLORATION_45)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")

        //what a scene editor is, and its history
        Subsection("What is a scene editor?")
        Paragraph("Most games are made by teams of people with different jobs:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("artists make the assets: pictures, 3D models and sounds;")
        ListItem("programmers write the code that decides how things behave;")
        ListItem("level designers put it all together into the places a player explores.")
        Paragraph("A scene editor is the level designer's tool. Instead of typing the position
of every wall and tree in code, a designer places them directly, moves them until they look right, and
saves the result as a scene that the game loads.")
        Paragraph("Scene editors grew out of the tools game studios built for themselves, and
some of the best known were given to players. StarCraft (1998) shipped with a map editor, and Warcraft III
(2002) shipped with its World Editor. Players used these to make thousands of their own maps, and some of
those maps grew into new kinds of games. Defense of the Ancients (DotA), which made the "):Quoted("battle arena"):Text("
genre popular, began as a map built in the Warcraft III World Editor. Today, scene editors are at the center
of general-purpose game engines such as Unity, Unreal Engine and Godot, which professional studios, students
and hobbyists all use. Making these tools available to everyone has changed who gets to make games.")
        Paragraph("This visual approach is common throughout computer science. In 3D modeling,
for example, tools like Maya or Blender let people shape one 3D object. Scene editors work with many
objects at once: a whole level, with everything in it.")

        //2D and 3D
        Subsection("2D and 3D scenes")
        Paragraph("Scenes come in two kinds.")
        Paragraph("A "):Term("2D"):Text(" scene is flat, like a page or a board. Items have a width and a height, but no depth.
2D scenes are common in:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("platform and puzzle games, and many phone games;")
        ListItem("top-down maps, such as an adventure game seen from above;")
        ListItem("board and card games;")
        ListItem("educational apps, animations and interactive stories.")
        Figure("media/scenes/CreateScene/Palette2D.png", "The 2D grid in the scene editor, with a row of green grass tiles and the Properties panel on the right.", "A 2D scene in Quorum Studio's scene editor.")
        Paragraph("2D scenes are often built from "):Term("tiles"):Text(": small square pictures, such as grass, water or walls, laid out on a grid like
floor tiles. Tools such as Tiled are used to lay out 2D maps this way.")

        Paragraph("A "):Term("3D"):Text(" scene has depth, so a camera can move through it and look around. 3D scenes are
used for:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("games set in worlds a player walks, drives or flies through;")
        ListItem("simulations, such as training to drive or fly;")
        ListItem("virtual reality;")
        ListItem("architecture, where people tour a building before it's built;")
        ListItem("science, where people explore a model of a molecule, a planet or a city.")
        Figure("media/scenes/CreateScene/Palette3D.png", "The 3D grid in the scene editor, with walls and a spaceship seen from above at an angle.", "A 3D scene in Quorum Studio's scene editor.")
        Paragraph("Neither kind is better. A 2D scene is often simpler to build and easier to
understand at a glance. A 3D scene can show the world the way we see it, but takes more work to build and
to explore. Quorum Studio's scene editor makes both.")

        //coordinates
        Subsection("Positions: x, y and z")
        Paragraph("Every item in a scene has a position, written as "):Term("coordinates"):Text(": one number for each direction.")

        Paragraph("In a "):Strong("2D"):Text(" scene, a position has two coordinates, "):Variable("x"):Text(" and "):Variable("y"):Text(":")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("x is how far to the right;")
        ListItem("y is how far up.")
        Paragraph("In Quorum, (0, 0) is the bottom-left corner. An item at (100, 50) is 100
units to the right of that corner and 50 units up. In a 2D scene, a unit is one pixel.")

        Paragraph("A "):Strong("3D"):Text(" scene adds a third coordinate, "):Variable("z"):Text(", for depth.")
        DescriptionList("The three directions")
        Description("x", "Left and right, as in 2D.")
        Description("y", "Up and down, as in 2D.")
        Description("z", "Toward and away from you. In Quorum, larger z values are farther away.")
        Paragraph("The point (0, 0, 0) is called the "):Term("origin"):Text(". In a 3D scene, one unit is one meter, the same as in the real world. A box at
(2, 0, 5) sits on the ground 2 meters to the right of the origin and 5 meters away from you.")
        Table("2D and 3D positions")
        TableHeader():Cell("Detail"):Cell("2D"):Cell("3D")
        TableRow():Cell("Coordinates"):Cell("x, y"):Cell("x, y, z")
        TableRow():Cell("One unit"):Cell("One pixel"):Cell("One meter")
        TableRow():Cell("Starting point"):Cell("(0, 0), the bottom-left corner"):Cell("(0, 0, 0), the origin")

        Paragraph("In code, a position is the same three numbers. This places a red box, 1 meter on
each side, at (2, 0, 5):")
        CodeExample("use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Model
use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Color

Color color
Model box
box:LoadBox(1, 1, 1, color:Red())
box:SetPosition(2, 0, 5)")
        Paragraph("You'll use these coordinates throughout this series, both in the scene
editor and in code.")

        //Quorum Studio
        Subsection("Scene editing in Quorum Studio")
        Paragraph("Quorum Studio's scene editor is in the tradition of these tools, except that
we obsess about making it all accessible to people with disabilities. Every part of it can be used from
the keyboard, and it tells a screen reader what is happening. For example, when you add an item, Quorum
Studio reports "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed"):DoubleQuote():Text(" followed by the item's name. You will see this throughout the series.")
        Paragraph("In this series, you will:")
        BulletList()
        ListItem("create a project with a scene in it;")
        ListItem("learn the parts of the scene editor;")
        ListItem("add, remove, move and change items;")
        ListItem("run a scene and change it from code;")
        ListItem("add terrain, the ground of a 3D world.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner build a real scene on a tabletop and
describe it with coordinates, the same way a scene editor does. You'll need a sheet of graph paper (or a
sheet of plain paper with a grid drawn on it), a ruler, and a handful of small objects, such as coins,
erasers, blocks, cups or game pieces. Pick one corner of the paper to be the origin, and mark it. Agree on
a unit: one square of graph paper, or one centimeter. The edge of the paper running right from the origin
is the x-axis, the edge running away from you is the z-axis, and straight up from the table is the y-axis.
If you don't have graph paper, mark the units along two edges with tape or a marker.")
        Paragraph("Together, place four to six objects on the paper to make a small scene, such
as a park, a room or a game level. Stack at least one object on another, so something sits above the
table. Then write down each object's name and its coordinates (x, y, z), measuring from the origin to the
center of its base. An object resting on the table has a y of 0. A coin on top of a block that is 3 units
tall has a y of 3. Next, test your description. One partner reads the list aloud while the other, with the
objects cleared away, rebuilds the scene from the coordinates alone. Compare the rebuilt scene with a photo
or sketch of the original. Wherever they differ, decide together whether the coordinate was wrong or
something was missing, such as which way an object faces or how big it is, and fix your list. Then swap roles.")
        TeachingTip()
        Paragraph("Students who can't see the tabletop can do the same activity by touch: use
objects with different shapes, and a grid made with raised lines, such as glue lines or wax sticks. The
partner reading the list can also describe each position out loud, the way Quorum Studio reports "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
        Paragraph("If time is short, use three objects, with one stacked on another.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("A scene editor turns a slow, error-prone job, typing the position of every
item, into one you can do directly. Behind the scenes, it is still keeping a list like the one you made in
Engage: every item, with its coordinates. What did your list need besides x, y and z to rebuild the scene
correctly?")
    end
end
